Azure Table Storage 
Why?
- It stores structured NoSQL data in the cloud.
- Fast and cost-effective
- Used to store flexible datasets
- Stores any number of entities in a table.
What?
Azure Table Storage is an Azure cloud service that allows us to store large amounts of structured data in a NoSQL key/value store.
Azure tables are ideal for storing structured, non-relational data.
When?
Large amounts of data : Azure Table Storage is used to store large amounts (terabytes) of data capable of serving web-scale applications.
De-normalized data : Azure table storage is used to store datasets that dont require complex joins, foreign keys, etc.
Fast access : Azure table storage is used in quickly querying data using a clustered index

Portal :
Storage account -> Data storage -> Tables -> +Table -> Table name


Azure Table Storage Concepts:

1. URL Format : Azure Table storage accounts use this url format to be accessed: 
	http://<storageaccount>.table.core.windows.net/<table>
2. Accounts : All access to azure storage is done via a storage account.
3. Tables : A table is a collection of entities.
4. Entities : An entity is a set of properties, like a database row.
5. Properties : A property is a key/value pair contained in an entity. An entity has three system properties: a partition key, a row key, and a timestamp. Entities with the same partition key can be queried more quickly.

Azure Table Storage Keys:
In Azure table storage eery entity has a primary key. This primary key is a composite key that is made up of two parts: Partition key and row key

Partition Key : 
A partition key is used to partition a table to support load balancing.
It is used to identify the partition that an entity belongs to.
Row Key:
A row key is used to uniquely identify an entity (record) in a given partition.
The partition key and the row key together form the primary key for an entity.

Microsoft Azure storage explorer is needed to add an entry in table. Can also add entries using storage browser in portal -> Storage account -> storage browser.




Azure Storage Queue
Portal -> Storage accounts -> Data storage -> Queue -> +queue -> name
Why?
Storage queues allow us to put messages in them, so other processes can read and process those messages; e.g., a message might contain the email address of a newly signed up user. Other processes can take messages from the queue and send those messages as emails.
What?
Azure queue storage is a service for storing large number of messages that can be accessed from anywhere in the world.
Concepts:
Accounts - All access to Azure storage is done via a storage account
URL Format - Queues are addressable using the following URL format: https://<storageaccount>.queue.core.windows.net/<queue>
Queues - A queue contains a set of messages. The queue name must be in lowercase.
Messages - A message can be in any format and up to 64kb. The maximum time-to-live allowed is any positive or -1 indicating that the message doesn't expire. the default timetolive is 7 days.
(FIFO)

